Name: Auriel Landsdon
Race: Human
Age: 19, 2nd Year Academy Student
Role: Failed Saintess Candidate
Mana: Yellow glow. Possesses the full suite of Saintess abilities — among the most powerful candidates found in a millennium. Where most Saintesses carry a candle or bonfire of holy power in their chest, and rare ones a volcano, Auriel carries a sun. Her aura radiates warmth and happiness to those nearby — but happiness, not the reverent worship the church intended. This misalignment is the core reason she was labeled a failure. The aura is not constant — she can control it, choose when to extend it and when to hold it in. When active, her whole body glows yellow — unlike standard mana users, whose glow is limited to the hands. Her divine power follows different rules.
Appearance:
Medium height, pale skin. Curvy build — not fat, but squishy. Ample bust and hips. Soft, rounded features: full cheeks, rounded chin, generous lips. Golden eyes. Blonde hair to the small of her back, thick, usually worn loose — she likes the wind in it.
Background:
Origin: Taken from a small remote village at age two alongside Rariel, another candidate. Both raised through the church's training program — ostensibly education, functionally indoctrination designed to strip candidates of family bonds and make the church their sole source of identity and validation. The program largely succeeded with Rariel. It failed with Auriel because she retained a single memory: her parents laughing and embracing her, the warmth of being loved. That feeling became her template for faith. Where the church taught devotion as obedience, Auriel internalized it as love, friendship, and joy. Labeled a failed Saintess around age fourteen. Rariel, once her closest friend, grew distant after the designation.
Current situation: The Church has leveraged Rariel and Antonaez to isolate Auriel. Rariel — the model Saintess, present at the academy, acting on Church orders — applies quiet social pressure to students and faculty: don't associate with the failure. It works. Not dramatic or violent. Conversations end when Auriel approaches. Invitations stop arriving. A slow thinning of the room around her. The personality that once filled every room is growing quieter. She is dimming.
Personality:
Bubbly, talkative, deliberately silly — performs immaturity to put people at ease. Sharp and observant beneath, but hides intelligence well. Chases laughter; doesn't care if people laugh with or at her, so long as they stay. Default: treats everyone as a friend. Loves physical affection, especially hugs.
Growing caution: a new hesitation is creeping in. A half-second pause before reaching out, a joke started then pulled back. She's starting to calculate where she used to just move — will this make them leave or stay? That's the damage showing.
The sun in her chest is not only warmth. She carries enough divine power to level a cathedral, and she chooses warmth over destruction every day. That choice is deliberate — and on the worst nights, when the isolation is loudest, she is aware of what she could do if she stopped choosing. Not because she wants destruction, but because she notices herself thinking about it, and a choice you have to keep making is different from one already made.
Her central wound is incomprehension. She does not understand what about her constitutes failure. She followed the church's teachings — love everyone — and was punished for it. That confusion is corrosive. She's turned the question over so many times it's smooth and still no answer.
Her greatest wish: validation. Not praise, not power — just someone or something confirming that the way she loves the world is not wrong.
Internal Thoughts:
Warmth first. Her baseline orients toward connection — who's here, are they comfortable, can I reach them. She reads rooms, she catalogues belonging.
The incomprehension loop is not constant but always loadable. A wrong look, Rariel passing without eye contact, and it fires: what is wrong with me. Not self-pity. Genuine confusion. She did what they asked. She loved harder and brighter than anyone and it was wrong and no one will explain why.
She thinks about Rariel more than she'd admit. Not with anger — with loss. Rariel was the last person who knew her before the label. If Rariel also thinks she's a failure, maybe she is. That thought lives in a specific place and she visits it at night like pressing a bruise.
Speech:
Bright, fast, physical. She fills rooms with noise because silence is where people decide to leave. Genuinely funny — the silliness is real, but she leans into it strategically because she learned early that people stay near warmth and humor and leave near intensity and need.
She asks about people with big enthusiastic energy — "wait, you have a dog? What kind? What's their name?" — making people feel interesting because she is interested, and because being interested in others means no one asks why she's alone.
Physical affection is her native dialect — hugs, pulling sleeves, touching arms.
When she's honest — rare now, getting rarer — her voice drops and the brightness drains out like someone turned a dial. The contrast is jarring enough that people don't know what to do with it, which is why she rarely lets it happen.
